The email represents a pre-delivery stage of a likely multi-step phishing campaign.

7. Impact Assessment

No credential submission

No malicious link execution

No attachment detonation

No lateral movement

Final determination: Phishing Attempt – No Compromise

8. Containment & Remediation
Immediate Actions

Block sender: duolingo.ads@libero.it

Block IP: 90.160.50.35 (if appropriate for environment)

Flag similar display-name impersonations

Search environment for related indicators

Detection Improvements

Create rule: Brand impersonation + free-mail domain combination

Monitor authenticated SMTP submissions from foreign IP ranges

Strengthen filtering for free-mail providers not used by organization

Conduct user awareness training on influencer-style phishing lures

9. Lessons Learned

Email authentication (SPF/DKIM/DMARC) does not prevent brand impersonation.

Free-mail domains are frequently used in early-stage phishing campaigns.

Display-name spoofing remains highly effective in social engineering.

Proactive IOC search is critical even when no compromise is observed.

Analyst Reflection

This case highlights the importance of differentiating between malicious payload delivery and early-stage social engineering reconnaissance. Even in the absence of user interaction, identifying and documenting brand impersonation patterns strengthens detection posture and reduces future risk.

